RUBRIC:{"title":"AERO9500 Report Marking Rubric (Assignment Part 2)","total":null,"criteria":[{"id":"C1","name":"Introduction: mission concept and rationale. Include the following required information: What is the spacecraft's primary mission? Why is the mission needed? How many spacecraft are used and why? Who are the users (operator and customers)?","weight":10,"max":null,"levels":[{"label":"Accomplished","min":7,"max":10,"desc":"Addresses all requirements to a high standard. Detailed descriptions and discussions are included. References to literature as appropriate."},{"label":"Adequate","min":4,"max":6,"desc":"Addresses some requirements. Descriptions may feature lack of specific details and/or incomplete referencing."},{"label":"Deficient","min":0,"max":3,"desc":"Many or all requirements are not addressed. Poor descriptions or lack of appropriate referencing."}]},{"id":"C2","name":"Main body: mission design. Present a summary of the mission design using software and/or hand calculations as appropriate. Include relevant screenshots and software files in the main body of the report and/or appendix. Include the following information: Where will it launch from, and using which launch vehicle? Why did you choose this option? What type of orbit will you use? What are its characteristics and orbital elements? Will you need to perform any orbital maneuvers? Which ground control facilities will you use? How often will your spacecraft be able to communicate with ground control? How long will the mission last, and what are your plans for EOL?","weight":60,"max":null,"levels":[{"label":"Accomplished","min":7,"max":10,"desc":"Demonstrates high level understanding of the design procedure. Design clearly addresses mission and course concepts. Major requirements are all addressed and are well supported with quality evidence (e.g. software screenshots, software files, hand calculations, references from literature). Content is comprehensive, accurate, and persuasive."},{"label":"Adequate","min":4,"max":6,"desc":"Demonstrates adequate understanding of the design procedure. Design may be inconsistent with regards to purpose and clarity of thought or feature errors. Major requirements are addressed but may lack some detail or are not well supported with evidence. Content may not be comprehensive and/or persuasive."},{"label":"Deficient","min":0,"max":3,"desc":"Demonstrates minimal understanding of the design procedure. Major requirements are not addressed and/or lack evidence. Content is incomplete or not persuasive."}]},{"id":"C3","name":"Conclusion","weight":10,"max":null,"levels":[{"label":"Accomplished","min":7,"max":10,"desc":"Detailed and appropriate conclusions reached from the evidence provided in the report."},{"label":"Adequate","min":4,"max":6,"desc":"Some conclusions are reached but they may be generic or lack specific detail."},{"label":"Deficient","min":0,"max":3,"desc":"No actual or useful conclusions reached."}]},{"id":"C4","name":"Report presentation","weight":20,"max":null,"levels":[{"label":"Accomplished","min":7,"max":10,"desc":"The document follows a clear and logical engineering report structure. The report is easy to read: well-written, with good spelling and grammar, and appropriate language style. All aspects of formatting are consistent throughout the document. Executive summary (or abstract) provides a concise overview of the report. Graphical and tabular presentation of data is appropriate, clear and consistent. References in text match reference list (and vice versa) and are cited properly."},{"label":"Adequate","min":4,"max":6,"desc":"Document is not at a professional level but does make use of headings and subheadings to indicate document structure. The report may be difficult to read: writing is just ok, broad idea comes across; spelling and grammar have some flaws, not quite appropriate language style. Although figures and tables are labelled, the formatting is unclear and/or inconsistent to the extent that the reader can lose track of the context when reading. References in text match reference list (and vice versa) and are mostly cited correctly."},{"label":"Deficient","min":0,"max":3,"desc":"The document is poorly structured, is not coherent or shows a lack of understanding of the purpose of its sections. Much effort is required to read and understand the report: writing is poor, many mistakes with spelling and grammar, and possibly inappropriate language style (e.g. too informal). Presentation is poor to the extent that it impedes reading of the document. Examples include inconsistent formatting, and unlabelled figures or tables. References are either not cited or cited inconsistently."}]}],"notes":"25% of course grade"}
TASK:{"title":"AERO9500 Final Report (Part 2): Space Mission Design","overview":"Develop a preliminary space-mission design that benefits Australia and report the mission concept and its space-segment elements. Use mission-design software of your choice, supported by hand calculations if useful, and justify the proposed design.","deliverables":["One technical-report PDF presenting the mission design and related work.","Relevant design-software screenshots and all relevant STK or PIGI files, included with the report or in an appendix.","If using software other than STK or PIGI, discuss it with the course contact before starting."],"format":{"length":"Maximum 20 pages for the core report; title page, table of contents, references and other non-core material are excluded.","structure":"Executive summary; Introduction (mission overview and rationale); Main body (mission space segment, including relevant launch, orbit, ground stations, coverage, operations and end-of-life sections); Conclusion; Bibliography.","other":"Use clear, effective engineering-report writing with correct grammar and spelling. Label figures and tables correctly with numbers, titles and references where needed. Cite and reference all material not originating with you; Wikipedia is not an acceptable source."},"requirements":[{"id":"R1","text":"Propose a simple mission that benefits Australia and explain its primary mission, overview and rationale."},{"id":"R2","text":"Specify whether the mission uses one or multiple spacecraft."},{"id":"R3","text":"Identify the mission's users, including operator and customers."},{"id":"R4","text":"State the launch site and launch vehicle."},{"id":"R5","text":"Describe the orbit, including its type, characteristics and orbital elements."},{"id":"R6","text":"State whether orbital manoeuvres are required and describe them as relevant."},{"id":"R7","text":"Identify ground-control facilities and explain how often the spacecraft can communicate with ground control."},{"id":"R8","text":"Specify mission duration and end-of-life plans."},{"id":"R9","text":"Use mission-design software; hand calculations may be added where useful. PIGI users must register for its competition to obtain a software licence."},{"id":"R10","text":"Support the mission design with relevant screenshots and provide all relevant STK or PIGI files."},{"id":"R11","text":"Ensure the report has a clear, logical structure and is concise; properly identify and cite all borrowed text, diagrams, ideas and other information."}]}
